"""Recursive CTE (WITH RECURSIVE): a walker performs the recursive union.

Seed row n=1 is the anchor term; each Row connects to its successor via a
typed ``NextStep`` edge. The walker accumulates ``total`` (UNION ALL) with an
explicit depth/termination policy mirroring the ``WHERE n < 100`` filter, so
the recursion is guaranteed to stop instead of spinning forever.
"""

node Row {
    has n: int = 0;
}

edge NextStep { }

walker RecursiveUnion {
    has total: int = 0;
    has max_n: int = 100;

    can start with Root entry {
        visit [->:NextStep:->];
    }

    can absorb with Row entry {
        # anchor/recursive union: accept the row if it satisfies the filter,
        # then keep expanding successors — explicit termination policy.
        if here.n <= self.max_n {
            self.total += here.n;
            visit [->:NextStep:->] else { disengage; }
        } else {
            disengage;
        }
    }
}

with entry {
    # Build chain 1 -> 2 -> ... -> 101 (101 proves the WHERE n < 100 cutoff).
    prev = Row(n=1);
    root ++> prev;
    for i in range(2, 102) {
        cur = Row(n=i);
        prev +>:NextStep:+> cur;
        prev = cur;
    }
    w = RecursiveUnion();
    root spawn w;
    print(f"WITH RECURSIVE t(n) sum = {w.total} (expected 5050)");
}

"""Support-graph removal ordering: a cycle-safe replacement for the Stage 5
non-terminating BFS. Objects are nodes, ``A supports B`` is a typed Support
edge, and a Remover walker computes removal levels with an explicit visited
guard so a support cycle is reported instead of looping forever."""

